</div>
<p>Chronologies of this shape recur across land-fraud FIRs: the civil suit comes first, the criminal complaint days later, and the criminal process then runs as leverage alongside the civil one. The order does not accuse this informant of that strategy — but the sequence plainly informed the Court\'s assessment that the accusation was, in nature, a civil dispute over sale and purchase of land.</p>

<h2>The anticipatory bail calculus in document-based offences</h2>
<p>Pre-arrest bail jurisprudence asks whether custodial interrogation is genuinely needed, whether the accused may flee or tamper, and how grave the accusation is. In offences built on registered documents, the evidence is largely already in official custody — the deed, the mutation records, the registers. The petitioners\' case was that the first petitioner was the bona fide recorded owner of the plot and competent to contract with the second petitioner as a bona fide purchaser; whether that is so will be decided by the civil court seized of the cancellation suit. Against that backdrop, with clean antecedents and a co-accused already on bail, the Court found the prayer justified, giving the protection a practical form: release on bail upon arrest or surrender within eight weeks, on bonds with sureties and statutory conditions.</p>

<h2>Civil dispute or crime? The working distinction</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Pointer towards a civil dispute</th><th>Pointer towards a genuine offence</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rival claims of title traceable to records; the dispute is over whose ownership is better</td><td>Fabricated or forged documents; signatures or identities impersonated</td></tr>
<tr><td>Civil proceedings over the same transaction instituted first</td><td>Criminal complaint discloses deception at the inception of the transaction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accusation restates the plaint\'s allegations with penal labels</td><td>Independent material: expert opinion on forgery, money trail, false personation before authorities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accused\'s conduct consistent with asserted ownership</td><td>Concealment, absconding, repeated similar transactions with strangers</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Relevance for Delhi practice</h2>
<p>Although this order comes from Patna, the pattern is familiar in Delhi\'s districts, where plot and floor transactions generate parallel FIRs at the Economic Offences Wing or local police stations while title suits and injunction applications proceed in the civil courts. The working lessons travel well: an accused seeking anticipatory bail should place the civil record — plaint, written statement, interim orders — before the bail court to show the dispute\'s true character; antecedents and parity with co-accused matter; and the statutory anchor is now Section 482 of the BNSS, which carries forward Section 438 CrPC, with conditions under Section 482(2) attaching to any grant.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Anticipatory bail protects liberty pending investigation; it neither decides title nor forecloses the investigation or trial. The civil suit over the sale deed remains pending before the Sub-Judge, and nothing in the order — or this note — bears on its merits.</p>
</div>
